# file ends in one and a stored statement does not.
#
# ⚠ BYTES AND CHARACTERS ARE BOTH HERE, AND THEY DIFFER ON NINE OF THE ELEVEN.
# Postgres `length()` counts CHARACTERS; `wc -c` counts BYTES; these files are
# commented in French and every accent is two bytes. Recording only the number
# production reported made the first version of the checker fail all nine, which
# is the friendly version of this mistake -- the unfriendly version is a check
# that passes on a file it never really compared.
#
# scripts/verify-recovered-migrations.sh checks it. It exists so that "these
# eleven files are exactly what ran" stays a fact rather than a memory: the
# eleven were never committed, were applied through apply_migration, and this
# hash is the only thing standing between them and a well-meaning tidy-up.
